<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tyne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tyne is a river of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, formed by the North and South Tyne near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexham">Hexham</a> and flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> and Gateshead into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> ran close to it, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the northern limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Durham's palatinate. It later became a great centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> shipping and shipbuilding.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tyne.jpg" alt="River Tyne" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilka Hudson from Newcastle, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newcastle_Quayside_with_bridges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
